---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
import coursework from "../data/coursework.json";

const files = coursework as { file: string; title: string; note: string }[];
const sections = [
  { id: "data", title: "The data" },
  { id: "curve", title: "Building the curve" },
  { id: "pricing", title: "Pricing a bond" },
  { id: "key-rates", title: "Key-rate durations" },
  { id: "hedge", title: "Hedging" },
  { id: "factors", title: "Curve factors (PCA)" },
  { id: "tree", title: "The rate tree" },
  { id: "convertible", title: "The convertible tree" },
  { id: "coursework", title: "The coursework models" },
  ...(files.length > 0 ? [{ id: "downloads", title: "The coursework itself" }] : []),
  { id: "engine", title: "The engine" },
];
---

<Doc
  title="How it works"
  kicker="Fixed income lab · methodology"
  description="How the fixed income lab builds the Treasury curve, prices and hedges bonds, finds the curve's factors, and values callable and convertible bonds, with every formula it uses."
  lede="Every number in the lab comes from bondlab, a Python fixed income library, through the same API as the options tool. This page sets out what it computes and how, in the order the lab uses it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="data">The data</h2>
  <p>
    The curve is the U.S. Treasury's <strong>Daily Treasury Par Yield Curve</strong>: for each business day, par yields at
    fixed maturities from one month to 30 years. The Treasury reads them off its own fitted curve of recently auctioned
    securities. Every day since 2 January 1990 is stored, and a scheduled job loads each new day twice an evening, so
    the lab never waits on treasury.gov.
  </p>
  <p>
    The set of published maturities has changed: no one-month bill before 2001, no 30-year bond from 2002 to early 2006,
    and new two-month, four-month and six-week bills since 2018. The lab builds each day from the maturities that day
    actually has and says which are missing. Choose a weekend or holiday and you get the last business day before it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="curve">Building the curve</h2>
  <p>Each published yield becomes an instrument the curve must price exactly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Six months or less: bills.</strong> One payment at maturity, the published yield read as a simple ACT/365
      rate, which is how the Treasury quotes bills of up to half a year.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Longer: par bonds.</strong> A semiannual Treasury issued on the curve date with a coupon equal to its
      published yield, so it is worth exactly 100.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The curve is <strong>bootstrapped</strong>: solved one maturity at a time, shortest first, each discount factor chosen
    so its instrument reprices to the quote with the earlier ones already fixed. Between pillars the curve needs an
    interpolation rule, and the lab offers three:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Monotone convex</td><td>Hagan and West's method, the one the Treasury itself uses: forwards stay positive and continuous, with no overshoot between pillars.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linear on zero rates</td><td>Straight lines between zero rates. Simple and local, but the forward curve jumps at every pillar.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Natural cubic spline</td><td>A smooth curve through the zero rates. The forwards look smooth too, but they can overshoot and wiggle between pillars.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The chart also shows a <strong>Nelson-Siegel</strong> fit to the same day's continuously compounded zero rates. It is
    a four-parameter curve, a level, a slope and a hump that decays at rate λ:
  </p>
  <pre>z(t) = β₀ + β₁·(1 − e^(−λt))/(λt) + β₂·[(1 − e^(−λt))/(λt) − e^(−λt)]</pre>
  <p class="where">
    All four parameters, λ included, are fitted by Levenberg-Marquardt least squares. Unlike the bootstrapped curve it
    does not pass through every point. The gap shows how much of the day's curve a smooth level-slope-curvature shape
    explains.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pricing">Pricing a bond</h2>
  <p>Two bonds are offered, and their conventions are stated, not hidden:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>US Treasury</th><th>USD corporate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Coupons</td><td>Semiannual</td><td>Semiannual</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accrual day count</td><td>ACT/ACT (ICMA)</td><td>30/360</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Settlement</td><td>T+1</td><td>T+2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Calendar</td><td colspan="2">SIFMA US bond market (Good Friday included)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The price can come from any one of three inputs, and the other two are solved from it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Off the curve.</strong> Each cash flow is discounted at the curve's semiannual zero rate plus a spread.</li>
    <li><strong>From a yield.</strong> The street-convention yield to maturity, compounded semiannually.</li>
    <li><strong>From a price.</strong> The yield and the Z-spread are solved to match the clean price you give.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre>dirty price = Σ CFᵢ · DF(tᵢ)        DF(t) = (1 + (z(t) + s)/2)^(−2t)
clean price = dirty price − accrued interest</pre>
  <p>
    Durations come from the yield: Macaulay is the present-value-weighted average time of the payments, and modified is
    Macaulay / (1 + y/2). Convexity measures how much the price curves against the yield. DV01 is the price change for a
    1 bp fall in yield, per 100 face. "Show your work" lists every cash flow with its discount factor and present value.
  </p>

  <h2 id="key-rates">Key-rate durations</h2>
  <p>
    A parallel DV01 treats the curve as one number. Key-rate durations split it by maturity. Each pillar's zero rate is
    moved up and then down by 1 bp on its own and the curve is rebuilt. The bond's change in value is the central
    difference:
  </p>
  <pre>KRDₖ = −[P(zₖ + 1bp) − P(zₖ − 1bp)] / 2</pre>
  <p class="where">
    The curve is re-fitted after each shock, so the shape of the bump between pillars is whatever the chosen
    interpolation implies, rather than Ho's imposed triangle. The two agree for a local scheme. The key-rate durations
    add up to about the parallel DV01, but not exactly, because several single-pillar shocks are not the same as one
    parallel shift.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hedge">Hedging</h2>
  <p>
    The position is the bond from "Price a bond". The hedges are Treasuries issued on the curve date at par, one per
    chosen maturity, priced off the same curve.
  </p>
  <p><strong>DV01 hedge:</strong> one bond, sized so a parallel 1 bp move leaves the total unchanged.</p>
  <pre>n = −DV01(position) / DV01(hedge)</pre>
  <p>
    <strong>Key-rate hedge:</strong> one hedge per chosen pillar. With A[t][h] the key-rate DV01 of hedge h at pillar t,
    and b[t] the position's, solve
  </p>
  <pre>A · n = −b</pre>
  <p>
    The hedged position then has zero DV01 at every chosen pillar. Unchosen pillars keep whatever exposure is left, and
    the lab shows it bar by bar. A set of hedges that cannot be solved, such as two hedges with the same exposure, is
    refused with the reason.
  </p>

  <h2 id="factors">Curve factors (PCA)</h2>
  <p>
    Litterman and Scheinkman (1991) found that almost all of the day-to-day movement of the Treasury curve is three
    shapes: a parallel <strong>level</strong> shift, a change of <strong>slope</strong>, and a change of
    <strong>curvature</strong>. The Factors tab finds those shapes in the stored data, over a window of 1, 2, 5 or 10
    years ending on the curve day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tenor set.</strong> The candidates are the ten maturities from 3 months to 30 years. A maturity is used
    only if the Treasury published it on every day of the window, and the tab names any it leaves out, with the first
    day it is missing. The stored data has three gaps that matter here. There is no 20-year yield before 1 October 1993,
    no 30-year from February 2002 to February 2006, and no 3-month on three days in December 2008. The 1-month and newer
    bills start too late (2001 to 2025) to use.
  </p>
  <p><strong>1. Changes.</strong> With Y the window's par yields (n days by m maturities), the daily changes in basis points are</p>
  <pre>ΔY = 10,000 · (Yₜ − Yₜ₋₁)      N = n − 1 changes</pre>
  <p class="where">A change across a weekend or a holiday counts as one day.</p>
  <p><strong>2. Covariance.</strong> Centre each column (X = ΔY minus its column means) and take the sample covariance in bp²:</p>
  <pre>S = XᵀX / (N − 1)</pre>
  <p><strong>3. Eigen-decomposition.</strong></p>
  <pre>S = V Λ Vᵀ      λ₁ ≥ λ₂ ≥ … ≥ λₘ</pre>
  <p class="where">
    Column vₖ is factor k's loading on each maturity, a unit vector. λₖ / Σλ is its share of the variance, and a typical
    day (one standard deviation) moves the curve by √λₖ · vₖ bp. An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so the lab fixes
    it to read naturally: level loadings sum to a positive number, slope is higher at the long end than the short end,
    and curvature is higher in the middle than at the two ends.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>4. How the factors moved.</strong> Each day's raw change is projected on each shape, ΔYₜ · vₖ, and the
    chart shows the running sums. They end at (Y<sub>end</sub> − Y<sub>start</sub>) · vₖ, the window's net curve move in
    that factor. Centred scores would always end at zero and hide it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>5. Par-yield DV01s.</strong> The factors are moves in published par yields, so a bond's exposure needs its
    sensitivity to each par yield, dₜ: the value lost for a 1 bp rise in the yield at maturity t, with the curve rebuilt.
    Rebuilding the curve once per maturity would cost a bootstrap each. The lab uses the chain rule instead. It shifts
    each zero-rate pillar s by a hundredth of a basis point and records how the bond's value moves (kₛ) and how each
    published yield moves (Jₜₛ), then solves one small system:
  </p>
  <pre>kₛ = Σₜ dₜ Jₜₛ      ⇒      Jᵀ d = k</pre>
  <p class="where">
    Bootstrapping is the inverse of reading quotes off the curve, so this equals rebuilding from bumped par yields to
    first order, and the tests check it against full rebuilds. The shift is a hundredth of a basis point because, with
    monotone convex interpolation, a full basis point at one pillar can trip the scheme's limiter.
  </p>
  <p><strong>6. Exposure.</strong> The P&amp;L of a typical day's move of factor k is</p>
  <pre>eₖ = −√λₖ · Σₜ dₜ vₖₜ</pre>
  <p>
    <strong>7. Level/slope hedge.</strong> Two par Treasuries at the chosen maturities, issued on the curve day as in the
    Hedge tab. With E[k][h] hedge h's exposure to factor k, the amounts solve
  </p>
  <pre>E · n = −e      (k = level, slope)</pre>
  <p>
    <strong>8. The comparison.</strong> The same two bonds are used four ways: no hedge; a DV01 hedge with the one
    nearer the bond's maturity; a key-rate hedge that zeroes the par-yield DV01 at the two maturities; and the
    level/slope hedge. For each, the daily risk left is the standard deviation of the hedged position's daily P&amp;L,
    replaying every day of the window through its DV01s:
  </p>
  <pre>σ = √(dᵀ S d)</pre>
  <p class="where">
    This uses every component, not just three, so it is the window's real history rather than the three-factor model
    judging itself. The key-rate row uses par-yield key rates, so it can differ slightly from the Hedge tab, which
    shifts zero rates.
  </p>
  <p class="where">
    Sources: Litterman, R. and Scheinkman, J. (1991), "Common Factors Affecting Bond Returns", <em>Journal of Fixed
    Income</em> 1(1); Jolliffe, I. T., <em>Principal Component Analysis</em>, 2nd ed., §1.1 and §2.1; Tuckman, B. and
    Serrat, A., <em>Fixed Income Securities</em>, 3rd ed., the chapter on empirical approaches to risk metrics and
    hedging.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tree">The rate tree</h2>
  <p>
    Callable and putable bonds are valued on a <strong>Kalotay-Williams-Fabozzi</strong> binomial tree of one-year rates.
    At each year the rates are spaced by the volatility:
  </p>
  <pre>r(i, j) = r(i, 0) · e^(2σj),   j = 0 … i</pre>
  <p>
    The lowest rate of each year is either typed in, as in the workbook, or calibrated so that the tree prices each par
    bond at exactly 100. The bond is then valued backward from maturity:
  </p>
  <pre>V(i, j) = ½ · [(V(i+1, j+1) + C) + (V(i+1, j) + C)] / (1 + r(i, j))
callable: V = min(V, call price)     putable: V = max(V, put price)</pre>
  <p class="where">
    The exercise check starts from the first exercise year. The option's value is the straight bond's value minus the
    callable bond's. On the workbook's inputs the tree gives 99.8150, the workbook's own answer; on the final exam's
    NVIDIA callable it gives 982.2212.
  </p>

  <h2 id="convertible">The convertible tree</h2>
  <p>
    A convertible lets its holder swap the bond for shares. The stock follows a <strong>Cox-Ross-Rubinstein</strong> tree
    with annual steps:
  </p>
  <pre>u = e^σ,   d = 1/u,   p = (e^(r − q) − d) / (u − d)
hold    = (p · V_up + (1 − p) · V_down) / e^r + coupon
convert = conversion ratio × S  (+ coupon, if the terms pay it on conversion)
V       = max(hold, convert)</pre>
  <p class="where">
    At maturity, holding is worth the face plus the last coupon. Today's node carries no coupon, because none is paid
    today. On the workbook's inputs the tree gives 1,504.0111, the workbook's answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coursework">The coursework models</h2>
  <p>
    The Basics area rebuilds the models from my fixed income derivatives course. Each opens on the workbook's own inputs
    and shows each workbook cell beside the number it gets. bondlab's tests check every one of those cells.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Workbook sheet</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Price, coupon and maturity effects</td><td>Coupon, Maturity</td><td>To 10⁻⁸</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Price over time, yield change</td><td>Time path, Yield Change</td><td>To 10⁻⁸</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duration and convexity</td><td>Duration and Convexity</td><td>To 10⁻⁶</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duration's tangent line</td><td>Tangency</td><td>To 10⁻⁶</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bootstrapping</td><td>Bootstrapping</td><td>To Goal Seek's precision (see below)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Callable bond on a rate tree</td><td>Binomial Tree; final exam Q1</td><td>To 10⁻⁸</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Convertible bond</td><td>Convertible Bond</td><td>To 10⁻⁸</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The workbook solved its bootstrapped spot rates with Goal Seek, which stops about 0.0001 percentage points short of
    the exact answer. bondlab solves exactly. Rebuilding the sheet also turned up one error in it. The three-year spot rate
    (the sixth semiannual period) reads a coupon from an empty cell (<code>=E22/2</code> where <code>=E13/2</code> was meant), which leaves the fourth
    coupon undiscounted and shows 6.7017% instead of 6.5543%. The lab gives the corrected value and says so beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The workbook's Black-Scholes sheet is not repeated here: the <a href="https://options.blessedshoh.dev">options tool</a>
    prices options with the same formula.
  </p>

  {files.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 id="downloads">The coursework itself</h2>
      <p>The workbooks the lab was built from, cleaned for posting:</p>
      <ul>
        {files.map((f) => (
          <li><a href={`/coursework/${f.file}`} download>{f.title}</a>. {f.note}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="engine">The engine</h2>
  <p>
    bondlab is written in Python with numpy and scipy, type-checked with mypy in strict mode, and tested against
    independent sources: the workbooks above, the Treasury's own published files, and textbook examples. Every formula
    in its code names its source. Conventions are never defaulted. A Treasury or a corporate bond is built from a named
    preset that writes its conventions out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The lab sends what is on screen to the API and draws what comes back. The browser does no finance of its own, so
    every number on screen can be reproduced by calling bondlab directly.
  </p>
</Doc>
